  "chapter": 505,
  "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
  "text": "\n \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 66 Chapter 505\n \n       MEMORANDUM filed with Senate Bill Number 6593-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to establish the task force on online lending institutions,\n        and providing for its powers and duties; and providing  for  the\n        repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  Online lending has become a popular financing option for consumers and\nsmall  businesses  that  have difficulty accessing capital from banks or\ncredit unions. Although online lending  institutions  may  offer  faster\naccess  to  funds, borrowing costs are typically higher. This bill would\nestablish a task force, supported by data from the Department of  Finan-\ncial  Services,  to  study  the industry, including the impact of online\nlending on consumers and small businesses and predatory  online  lending\npractices.\n \n  The Department of Financial Services is the sole regulator of non-bank\nlenders  in  the  State. Accordingly, the Department has already identi-\nfied, through its own analysis as well as input from  interested  stake-\nholders,  several  proposals  that  are  necessary to foster innovation,\nensure a level playing field, and above all, protect New York consumers.\n \n  Consistent with its regulatory authority, the Department must continue\nto have the flexibility to implement such proposals based on its  exper-\ntise  and  knowledge  of  the  industry.  Thus rather than create a task\nforce, the Executive has secured an agreement with  the  Legislature  to\npass  legislation in the upcoming session that would require the Depart-\nment to utilize its expertise to continue  studying  this  industry  and\nthen  issue  a summary report. Specifically, in consultation with inter-\nested stakeholders, the Department will examine and make recommendations\nconcerning online lending institutions presently operating in the state,\nincluding licensure, products and services offered, disclosure and lend-\ning practices, interest rates and fees charged. The Department will also\nsurvey applicable laws and regulations, and whether certain  regulations\nwould need to be advanced in a more expeditious manner to curb predatory\npractices. The Department will issue a report detailing its findings and\nrecommendations  on  or before July 1, 2018. On this basis, I am signing\nthis bill.\n \n  This bill is approved.                      (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                                ______\n",
  "year": 2017
